    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-73565",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix",
      "problem": "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The lines $2x+4y=0$ and $3x+6y=0$ intersect at exactly one point\n\nIII. ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 6-4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem: \n\n- Statement II is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
