    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-128154",
      "knowledge_point_id": "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing a True Statement",
      "problem": "Consider the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is singular"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is the solution to $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has \n no solution"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "$[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-128154",
        "kp_title": "Completing a True Statement",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-31919",
      "knowledge_point_id": "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing a True Statement",
      "problem": "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det(A)\\ne 0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, then $A$ is $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. invertible\n\nII. **not ** invertible\n\nIII. singular",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option I gives a true statement:\n\n- *If $det(A)\\ne 0$ , then $A$ is invertible.* $\\,✓$",
