  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4511",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d49a7b78-bea1-54e5-8232-3fea0d4eb5a3",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a True Statement Concerning a 2x2 System of Equations",
      "problem": "Consider a system of two linear equations in two variables, represented by the matrix equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ in the usual way. Given that the system has exactly one solution, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) =0$\n\n- The system ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/0b4ad54b702a4c7dc8cca849c32d035e558a8b91d8df22b14eb65b28f41093b0.png) has exactly one solution\n\n- $A^{-1}$ does not exist",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/79f95782298c108b174ce190af7b3a073fa0f704072932967c4f16313c955ce7.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4512",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying a True Statement Given a 2x2 Matrix",
      "problem": "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/15ba3333a2ced8790efd81a35d26be43d2f0841b9529bf9d6b5b5e1c0e9f9b67.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/18978e96f80b0ba3d0f997c1db6162886b9d9e2d2c5ee7d5925a48b6147081af.png) where $k\\neq 4$ is a constant. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has no solutions\n\n- The lines $x-2y=1$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at exactly one point",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/43da43be5ab47c640387f77d3b61555d79e9b9a2d61f49d00b56e45a6856bdec.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =k-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k - 4 \\neq 0$ if $k \\neq 4.$ \n\nSo, since the fifth part in the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the second part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be exactly one solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4513",
      "knowledge_point_id": "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04",
      "knowledge_point": "Completing a True Statement",
      "problem": "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has infinitely many solutions\n\n- $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14076826/assets/db710af5540e8c811c83c12c23a5f5289539fc8628a936c183badc00933b2068.png), then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines $a_{11}x+a_{12}y=b_1$ and $a_{21}x+a_{22}y=b_2$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $\\det(A) \\neq 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option II gives us a true statement:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has exactly one solution.* $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$",
